course: space
lesson: 14
title: Uranus and Neptune
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Uranus and Neptune are the ice giants: under their hydrogen and helium air they are mostly water, ammonia and methane squeezed into a hot, dense slush.
explain: Methane in their air swallows red light, so the light that bounces back to us is blue-green for Uranus and blue for Neptune.
explain: Uranus lies on its side, tipped over by about 98 degrees, so it rolls round the Sun like a ball rolling along the floor rather than a spinning top.
explain: Neptune was found with a pencil before a telescope: mathematicians saw Uranus being tugged off course, worked out where the hidden planet must be, and astronomers found it there in 1846.
explain: Both are so far away that only one spacecraft has ever visited them, Voyager 2, in 1986 and 1989.
fact: Uranus and Neptune are called ice giants because, under hydrogen and helium air, they are mostly water, ammonia and methane in hot, dense slushy forms.
fact: Uranus and Neptune look blue-green and blue because methane gas in their air absorbs red light.
fact: Uranus was the first planet discovered with a telescope, found by William Herschel in Bath, England, in 1781.
fact: Uranus is tipped over by about 98 degrees, so it rolls round the Sun on its side.
fact: Uranus has the coldest measured atmosphere of any planet, about minus 224 degrees Celsius, even though Neptune is further out.
fact: Uranus has 13 narrow dark rings and more than 25 known moons.
fact: Uranus's moons are named after characters from Shakespeare and Alexander Pope, such as Miranda, Titania and Oberon.
fact: Neptune was found in 1846 after mathematicians predicted where it must be from the way it tugged on Uranus.
fact: Neptune has the fastest winds in the Solar System, about 2,000 kilometres per hour.
fact: Neptune takes about 165 Earth years to orbit the Sun, so it completed its first orbit since discovery only in 2011.
fact: Neptune's largest moon Triton orbits backwards and is probably a captured object from the Kuiper belt, a ring of icy little worlds beyond Neptune.
fact: Uranus and Neptune have each been visited only once, by Voyager 2, in 1986 and 1989.
exercise: Why are Uranus and Neptune called ice giants? | They are mostly water, ammonia and methane in hot, dense forms | They are covered in frozen ice-skating rinks | They are frozen solid all the way through, like giant snowballs in space | They are the smallest and coldest planets
exercise: Why do Uranus and Neptune look blue? | Methane in their air absorbs red light | They are covered with deep oceans | They reflect the blue of the Earth | They are hotter than the other planets
exercise: Who discovered Uranus, and where? | William Herschel, in Bath, England | Galileo Galilei, in Padua, northern Italy | Clyde Tombaugh, at Flagstaff in Arizona | Isaac Newton, in Cambridge, England
exercise: What was special about the discovery of Uranus in 1781? | It was the first planet found with a telescope | It was the first planet found by a space probe | It was the last planet known to the Romans | It was found with the naked eye
exercise: How is Uranus tipped? | By about 98 degrees, so it rolls on its side | Not at all, it stands straight up | By about 23 degrees, like the Earth | Upside down, by exactly 180 degrees
exercise: Which planet has the coldest measured atmosphere? | Uranus | Neptune | Mercury | Mars
exercise: About how cold is Uranus's atmosphere at its coldest? | About minus 224 degrees Celsius | About minus 20 degrees Celsius | About 0 degrees Celsius | About minus 2,000 degrees Celsius
exercise: How many rings does Uranus have? | 13 narrow dark rings | None at all | One wide bright ring | Thousands of bright rings
exercise: After whom are Uranus's moons named? | Characters from Shakespeare and Alexander Pope | Greek gods and goddesses | Famous astronomers who studied the planet with telescopes | Kings and queens of England
exercise: How was Neptune found in 1846? | Mathematicians predicted where it must be from its tug on Uranus | A space probe photographed it | Sailors saw it over the sea | It was seen by chance with the naked eye
exercise: Which planet has the fastest winds in the Solar System? | Neptune, about 2,000 kilometres per hour | Jupiter, about 100 kilometres per hour | The Earth, about 400 kilometres per hour | Mars, about 5,000 kilometres per hour
exercise: How long does Neptune take to orbit the Sun? | About 165 Earth years | About 29 Earth years | About 1 Earth year | About 1,000 Earth years
exercise: When did Neptune complete its first orbit since it was discovered? | In 2011 | In 1846 | In 1900 | It has not finished one yet
exercise: What is odd about Neptune's moon Triton? | It orbits backwards | It is bigger than Neptune | It has no gravity | It is made of iron
exercise: Where did Triton probably come from? | It was captured from the Kuiper belt | It broke off the Earth in a huge crash long ago | It formed inside Neptune's deep slushy layers | It was pushed there by the Sun's strong wind
exercise: Which spacecraft is the only one to have visited Uranus and Neptune? | Voyager 2 | Cassini | New Horizons | Apollo 11
exercise: In which years did Voyager 2 pass Uranus and Neptune? | 1986 and 1989 | 1969 and 1972 | 2015 and 2019 | 2004 and 2017
test: Ben says Neptune must be the coldest planet cos it's furthest out. true? | No, Uranus has the coldest measured air | Yes, the furthest is always coldest | No, Mercury is the coldest | Yes, Neptune is below minus 2,000 degrees
test: In a game, Uranus spins standing straight up like a top. How should it really spin? | Tipped right over on its side | Upside down and spinning very fast | Not at all, it stays perfectly still | Exactly like the Earth, a little tilted
test: A teacher pours an ice giant into a glass in a cartoon, and out comes snow. what's wrong? | Inside it is hot, dense slush, not cold snow | Nothing, it is all snow inside | It should be liquid iron | It should be empty space
test: If Uranus had no methane in its air, how would its colour change? | It would lose most of its blue-green colour | It would turn much bluer and darker than before | It would turn bright red | It would glow like a star
test: My nan's 80 and says Neptune hasn't gone round the Sun once in her life. is she right? | Yes, it needs about 165 years for one orbit | No, it goes round once a year | No, it has gone round about 80 times | Yes, because Neptune does not move at all
test: you're joking, a planet found with maths?? how | Uranus was pulled off course, so they worked out where the hidden planet was | Someone guessed a random spot and got lucky | A probe found it and the maths came later | Mathematicians saw it through the first telescope
test: Which moon of Uranus would you find in a Shakespeare play? | Miranda | Ganymede | Callisto | Enceladus
test: Why do we know so little about Uranus and Neptune? | Only one spacecraft has ever visited them | No telescope on Earth has ever been able to see them | They are hidden behind the Sun | They were only discovered last year
test: Which is true? | Triton orbits Neptune backwards and was probably captured | Triton is Uranus's largest moon | Triton formed at the same time as the Earth | Triton has the fastest winds in the Solar System
test: Neptune's winds are about 2,000 kilometres per hour. A fast passenger jet flies about 900. The winds are | More than twice as fast as the jet | About half as fast as a passenger jet | Exactly as fast as the jet | Ten times slower than the jet
test: crap, forgot which ice giant Herschel found. help | Uranus, in 1781 | Neptune, in 1846 | Saturn, in 1610 | Pluto, in 1930
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the planets Uranus and Neptune present a blue colouration. | The absorption of red light by atmospheric methane | The reflection of sunlight from vast oceans of liquid water | The reflection of light from the Earth | Their exceptionally high surface temperatures
exam: State the method by which the existence and position of Neptune were first established in the year eighteen hundred and forty-six. | Mathematical prediction from perturbations in the orbit of Uranus | Photography by a space probe | Observation with the unaided eye | Detection by radio transmission
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the inclination of Uranus. | Its axis is tilted by approximately ninety-eight degrees | Its axis is perfectly upright | Its axis is tilted by precisely twenty-three and a half degrees | It possesses no axis of rotation
exam: Ascertain the number of spacecraft which have hitherto visited the planets Uranus and Neptune. | One, namely Voyager 2 | Four, namely the Voyager and Pioneer craft | None whatsoever, as both are too distant | Twelve, launched by several nations
